ac{1}{2}z=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}z{e}^{-x/2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-x/2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-x/2} & =-{e}^{-x/2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =C{e}^{x/2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2}=C{e}^{x/2}-1\\,⇒\\,y={(C{e}^{x/2}-1)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350779 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350780 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350781 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350782 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350783 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1041 "selection" 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350782 13194139535469 true] [17592186350778 1110 #uuid "ff790ca9-21da-56ed-8672-32ce810cbd5e" 13194139535469 true] [17592186350779 1034 "y={(\\frac{x}{C{e}^{x/2}-1})}^{2}" 13194139535469 true] [17592186350779 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194